Q: How does reading data with &gt;&gt; work inside a while loop in C++? A: <p>When used with stream I/O, the <code>&gt;&gt;</code> operator takes in (a reference to) a stream on the left for input, and (a reference to) a variable on the right for output. It returns (a reference to) the input stream, to allow chaining. Which is what the condition expression of your <code>while</code> loop is doing - the return value of one expression is the input of the next chained expression.</p>
<p>In the expression <code>File&gt;&gt;l&gt;&gt;x&gt;&gt;w&gt;&gt;x&gt;&gt;h</code>, the compiler breaks it up into smaller sub-expressions, eg:</p>
<p><code>File &gt;&gt; l</code><br />
<code>... &gt;&gt; x</code><br />
<code>... &gt;&gt; w</code><br />
<code>... &gt;&gt; x</code><br />
<code>... &gt;&gt; h</code></p>
<p><code>File &gt;&gt; l</code> is evaluated first. <code>l</code> is an <code>int</code> and there is an overload of the <code>&gt;&gt;</code> operator for <code>int</code> output. It reads from <code>File</code>, writes the output <code>int</code> value to <code>l</code>, and returns a reference to <code>File</code>.</p>
<p>Then <code>... &gt;&gt; x</code> is evaluated. <code>x</code> is a <code>char</code> and there is an overload of the <code>&gt;&gt;</code> operator for <code>char</code> output. Its left-hand input is the stream reference returned by the previous expression. It reads from <code>File</code>, writes the output value to <code>x</code>, and returns a reference to <code>File</code>.</p>
<p>And so on for the remaining sub-expressions.</p>
<p>After <code>... &gt;&gt; h</code> is evaluated, there is nothing more to call. Its return value (a stream reference) gets passed to the <code>while</code> statement, which is expecting a <code>bool</code> value. <code>File</code> is obviously not a <code>bool</code>, but the <code>std::basic_istream</code> class defines a conversion operator to <code>bool</code>, which returns <code>false</code> if the stream is in an error state, otherwise it returns <code>true</code>.</p>
<p>So, if all of the <code>&gt;&gt;</code> operator calls were successful, <code>File</code> will not be in an error state, so it converts to <code>true</code>, and the <code>while</code> loop body is entered, which can safely use the values of the <code>l</code> <code>x</code> <code>w</code> <code>h</code> variables.</p>
<p>Otherwise, if any <code>&gt;&gt;</code> operator call failed (ie EOF was reached, or bad data conversions were encountered, etc), <code>File</code> will be in an error state, so it will convert to <code>false</code>, and the <code>while</code> loop will break.</p>
<p>Basically, the compiler will treat the <code>while</code> loop <em>as-if</em> you had written your code more like this (this is just representative, not exact):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;fstream&gt;
using namespace std;

int main(){

    fstream File(&quot;input.txt&quot;);

    string line;
    float surface_area ;
    char x;
    int l;
    int w;
    int h;

    do {
        // the '&gt;&gt;' operator for 'int' is a class member of std::basic_istream,
        // but the '&gt;&gt;' operator for 'char' is a free-standing function instead...
        istream &amp;ref1 = File.operator&gt;&gt;(l);
        istream &amp;ref2 = ::operator&gt;&gt;(ref1, x);
        istream &amp;ref3 = ref2.operator&gt;&gt;(w);
        istream &amp;ref4 = ::operator&gt;&gt;(ref3, x);
        istream &amp;ref5 = ref4.operator&gt;&gt;(h);

        if (!ref5.operator bool()) break;

        cout &lt;&lt; l &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; w &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; h;
    }
    while (true);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
